if (nameoff < sizeof(struct erofs_dirent) || nameoff >= bsz) { ... }

Reason:
The first statement computes `maxsize - nameoff` without verifying that `nameoff` is less than `maxsize`. A crafted trailing dirent with `nameoff >= maxsize` causes unsigned underflow, producing a huge length, which leads `strnlen` to read out-of-bounds memory beyond the directory block. The second statement only checks the lower bound against `sizeof(struct erofs_dirent)` but does not enforce alignment to that size, allowing further structural inconsistencies (e.g., unaligned nameoff0) that can trigger the same underflow or other issues.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
else if (maxsize <= nameoff)
    goto err_bogus;
else
    de_namelen = strnlen(de_name, maxsize - nameoff);
if (!nameoff || nameoff >= bsz || (nameoff % sizeof(*de))) { ... }

Reason:
The first fix adds a bounds check (`maxsize <= nameoff`) before the subtraction, preventing the unsigned underflow and the subsequent out-of-bounds read. It now correctly handles the trailing dirent case by validating `nameoff` against `maxsize` and jumping to an error label if invalid. The second fix improves the initial `nameoff0` validation by also checking that `nameoff` is not zero and is a multiple of `sizeof(struct erofs_dirent)`, ensuring proper alignment and catching corrupted directory structures earlier.

